---
/* GENERATED from patterns.json — the roadmap item (112.5) made this a
   condition, and the reason is worth restating: a hand-maintained decision
   flow would be a FIFTH interpretation of the pattern system, free to drift
   from the sidebar, the tile index, the pages themselves and pattern-groups.
   Everything below is read from the same file `/patterns/` is built from.
   Nothing here is typed twice.

   The three things extracted are the three a reader actually chooses by:
   the job family (the group), who the screen is for, and — most usefully —
   the pattern's own statement of when it is the WRONG choice. That last one
   is enforced to exist by `check:wrong-choice`, so it is a reliable source
   rather than a lucky one.

   The heading below carries the same `generated` badge ApiTable/ClassRef/
   DsaScore use, added 2026-08-28: this is the longest single block in the docs
   (2,014 of the page's 2,324 words) and not one of those words is typed here,
   so a reader — and `report:prose`, which counts the badge — should be able to
   tell that without reading this comment (roadmap 158.1). */
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
import patterns from '../../data/patterns.json';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

const tiles = patterns.groups.flatMap((g: any) => g.tiles);
const byTitle = new Map<string, any>(tiles.map((t: any) => [t.title.toLowerCase(), t]));

const WHO = /Who uses it:\s*(.+?)(?=\s+(?:How often|What "done")|$)/s;
const NOT = /\b(?:Not for|Not when)\b\s*(.+?)(?=\s*(?:\.\s+[A-Z]|$))/s;

/** Split a wrong-choice clause so any pattern it names becomes a real link. */
function linkAlternatives(clause: string, selfTitle: string) {
  const hit = [...byTitle.entries()].find(
    ([lt]) => lt !== selfTitle.toLowerCase() && clause.toLowerCase().includes(lt),
  );
  if (!hit) return { before: clause, target: null as any, after: '' };
  const [lt, target] = hit;
  const i = clause.toLowerCase().indexOf(lt);
  return { before: clause.slice(0, i), target, after: clause.slice(i + lt.length) };
}

const groups = patterns.groups.map((g: any) => ({
  label: g.label.replace(/^Patterns:\s*/, ''),
  rows: g.tiles.map((t: any) => {
    const who = WHO.exec(t.opener)?.[1]?.trim() ?? '';
    const notRaw = NOT.exec(t.opener)?.[1]?.trim() ?? '';
    return { ...t, who, not: notRaw ? linkAlternatives(notRaw, t.title) : null };
  }),
}));

const withNot = groups.flatMap((g: any) => g.rows).filter((r: any) => r.not).length;
const withWho = groups.flatMap((g: any) => g.rows).filter((r: any) => r.who).length;
---
<Gallery title="Which pattern should I use?" description="Pick a screen pattern by the job someone is trying to finish, not by the widget. Every pattern says in its own words when it is the wrong choice.">
  <p class="demo-note">
    Pick by the JOB, not by the widget. A screen's pattern follows from what
    the person is trying to finish — find something, work one record, enter
    data, clear a queue, watch something run — and every pattern below says in
    its own words when it is the <em>wrong</em> choice, which is usually the
    faster way to decide. <strong>Not a component index</strong>: if you know
    the screen and need the parts, the pattern page lists them, and
    <a href={base + '/components/button'}>the component pages</a> document each
    one. This page only answers "which screen am I building?".
  </p>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Start from the job <span class="bo-badge">generated</span></h2>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Six families, in the order a working day runs
    through them. Every row is read from the same file that builds
    <a href={base + '/patterns/'}>the pattern index</a> and the sidebar, so
    this page cannot disagree with them about what exists.</p>

    {groups.map((g: any) => (
      <>
        <h3>{g.label}</h3>
        <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0" data-density="compact">
          <table class="bo-data-table">
            <caption class="bo-visually-hidden">{g.label} — patterns, who they serve, and when not to use them</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Pattern</th>
                <th scope="col">Reach for it when</th>
                <th scope="col">Not when</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {g.rows.map((r: any) => (
                <tr>
                  <td><a class="bo-data-table__cell-link" href={base + r.href}>{r.title}</a></td>
                  <td>{r.who || <span class="bo-u-text-muted">—</span>}</td>
                  <td>
                    {r.not ? (
                      <>
                        {r.not.before}
                        {r.not.target && (
                          <a href={base + r.not.target.href}>{r.not.target.title}</a>
                        )}
                        {r.not.after}
                      </>
                    ) : (
                      <span class="bo-u-text-muted">—</span>
                    )}
                  </td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>How this page is built, and what it does not cover</h2>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Every cell above is extracted from
    <code>patterns.json</code>, which is generated from the pattern pages
    themselves. Adding a pattern adds a row here with no edit to this file;
    rewording a page's opener rewords its row. That is the point — a
    hand-written decision flow would be a fifth place the pattern system is
    described, free to drift from the other four.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>Stated so you can see the gaps rather
    than trust the table.</strong> Of {tiles.length} patterns,
    <strong>{withWho}</strong> state who they are for and
    <strong>{withNot}</strong> state when not to use them; the rest show
    <span class="bo-u-text-muted">—</span> and their page is the answer. The
    "not when" column is the pattern's own wrong-choice clause, which
    <code>check:wrong-choice</code> requires every page to carry — so a blank
    means the clause is phrased in a way this extraction did not match, never
    that the page is silent on it.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">And a pattern named in a wrong-choice clause is
    linked automatically where the clause names one outright. Where it
    describes the alternative instead of naming it, the prose stands — the
    page it points at is one sentence away, and inventing a link the author did
    not write would be this page guessing.</p>
  </section>

  <Related links={[
    ['/patterns/', 'All patterns'],
    ['/concepts/design-language', 'Design language'],
    ['/getting-started/first-screen', 'Build your first screen'],
  ]} />
</Gallery>
